{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"446ef5d2\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.\n- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.\n\nRule:\n- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\n- Translate pieces (no rotation) to tile the rectangle; touching edges must match, with rect_col as wildcard.\n\nKey insight: rect_col acts as wildcard during edge matching — only non-rect_col pixels at shared boundaries must agree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5961cc34\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nBuild a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.\n- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.\n\nRule:\n- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.\n- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.\n- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.\n\nKey insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f931b4a8\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nCount the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.\n- Black is the background.\n- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.\n- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.\n\nRule:\n- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.\n- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.\n- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.\n- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.\n\nKey insight:\n- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"35ab12c3\" lines=\"73-88\">\n<description>\nComplete outlined shapes and carry attached shadow pixels along their edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Each active color marks vertices of an 8-direction aligned outline.\n- A start vertex can have adjacent non-bg, different-colored shadow pixels.\n- Other colored pixels may be distractors unless they lie on valid drawable edges.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, order its pixels by polar angle around their centroid to form a cycle.\n- Draw only horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree edges whose interiors are clear.\n- From the chosen start vertex, copy adjacent shadow offsets along each drawable edge\n  when the parallel path is in bounds and clear.\n\nKey insight: The main outline is reconstructed first, then the start vertex's neighbor offsets define parallel shadow strokes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f3a5a89\" lines=\"22-36\">\n<description>\nPaint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\n- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\n- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\n- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\n- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\n\nKey insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5545f144\" lines=\"191-207\">\n<description>\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.\n- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the rightmost frame.\n- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable\n  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the\n  nearest one.\n- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.\n- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
